# ---------------- Rapa Nui ----------------
meta={
"type":"place","title_zh":"复活节岛：石像、祖先与一座岛的账","title_en":"Rapa Nui: Stone Ancestors and an Island's Ledger",
"era":"约 1250–1888 年","era_en":"c. 1250–1888 CE",
"region":"智利，瓦尔帕莱索大区，复活节岛","region_en":"Easter Island, Valparaíso Region, Chile",
"coords":[-27.1258,-109.2767],"tags":["复活节岛","摩艾","拉帕努伊","智利","波利尼西亚"],"collections":[],"credibility":"verified",
"related":["machu-picchu","sacsayhuaman"],
"en_headings":["An island of stone figures","Quarry, transport, and the 'walking' debate","Ahu platforms and ancestor cult","Ecology, population, and the collapse story","Colonial contact and dispersal","National park, community, and return requests"],
"limits_zh":"复活节岛的年代框架、人口规模与生态变化仍有争论。摩艾的运输方式没有一种被完全证实；所谓“生态自杀”与“外来疾病和奴役导致崩溃”两种叙事，各自选择不同的证据。石像的数量、哪些曾被竖立、哪些留在采石场，也随调查方法变化。本文把可测的石材、平台和档案记录放在前面，把“消失的文明”式概括放在解释层，并明确标注仍属假说的部分。",
"limits_en":"The chronology, population size, and ecological history of Rapa Nui remain debated. No transport method for the moai has been fully demonstrated, and the 'ecological suicide' and 'disease and raiding collapse' narratives each select different evidence. The number of figures, which were erected, and which remained at the quarry also change with survey methods. This entry puts measurable stone, platforms, and archival records first, treats 'lost civilisation' summaries as interpretation, and flags what remains hypothesis.",
}
sections=[
("一、石像之岛","复活节岛在太平洋东南部，面积约 163 平方公里，距智利本土约 3500 公里，是地球上最偏远的有人居住岛屿之一。岛上已记录的摩艾石像接近千尊，多数用火山凝灰岩雕刻，集中在拉诺拉拉库采石场附近；一部分被竖立在称为“阿胡”的石台上，另一部分仍半埋在山坡。石像不是统一的“头像”：有的只有头部，有的连身体，有的头上还顶着红色的普卡奥发髻。","Easter Island lies in the south-eastern Pacific, about 163 square kilometres in area and some 3,500 kilometres from mainland Chile, making it one of the most remote inhabited islands on Earth. Nearly a thousand moai have been recorded, most carved from volcanic tuff and concentrated around the Rano Raraku quarry; some were erected on stone platforms called ahu, while others remain half-buried on the slope. The figures are not uniform 'heads': some show only a face, some include a body, and some wear a red pukao topknot."),
("二、采石、运输与“走路”之争","摩艾主要在拉诺拉拉库的凝灰岩崖壁上雕刻，再由 teams 运往海岸。运输方式争论了一个多世纪：木橇与滚木、绳索牵引、独木舟式滑运，以及 2012 年实验提出的“直立摇晃行走”模型。每种方案都能在局部试验中移动复制品，却难以解释全部路线、坡度和石像尺寸。岛上没有足够的大树制造滚木，这使“滚木运输”假说必须面对资源约束；但“走路”模型也需要平坦路面和大量人力。","The moai were carved mainly from the tuff cliffs of Rano Raraku and then moved by teams toward the coast. Transport has been argued for more than a century: sledges and rollers, rope hauling, canoe-like sliding, and the 'vertical rocking walk' tested in 2012. Each method can move a replica over a short course, but none easily explains all routes, slopes, and figure sizes. The island lacked enough large trees for rollers, which puts the roller hypothesis under resource pressure; the walking model also needs prepared surfaces and many hands."),
("三、阿胡、祖先与鸟人","竖立的摩艾通常背对海洋、面向村落，立在阿胡平台上。平台既是墓室，也是仪式中心：石像代表祖先，面向生者，承担保护与丰产的功能。岛上另有“鸟人”崇拜，集中在奥龙戈的悬崖村落；每年比赛收集乌燕鸥蛋，胜者获得“ tangata manu ”称号。祖先崇拜与鸟人仪式并不矛盾，它们共同构成一个以血缘、季节和资源为中心的社会。","Erected moai usually face inland toward the village, with their backs to the sea, standing on ahu platforms. The platform was both tomb and ritual centre: the figure represented an ancestor, faced the living, and carried protective and fertility functions. The island also had a Birdman cult centred on the cliff village of Orongo; each year competitors collected sooty tern eggs, and the winner became tangata manu. Ancestor veneration and Birdman ritual were not contradictory; together they formed a society organised around lineage, season, and resources."),
("四、生态、人口与崩溃叙事","岛上的森林在人类到来后大幅减少，老鼠、火灾、农耕和木材使用都可能参与其中。一种流行叙事说，岛民为了竖立石像砍光森林，最终陷入饥荒和内战；另一种研究强调，土壤侵蚀和干旱有自然因素，人口下降也与欧洲人带来的疾病、奴隶 raid 和殖民压力有关。两种解释并不互相取消。可以确认的是，森林确实减少，社会确实在 19 世纪经历剧变；不能确认的是，单一原因能在什么时间尺度上解释全部变化。","The island's forests shrank sharply after human arrival, with rats, fire, farming, and timber use all likely contributing. One popular narrative says islanders cut down the last trees to erect figures and then fell into famine and war; another line of research stresses natural soil erosion and drought, and links population decline to diseases, slave raids, and colonial pressure brought by Europeans. The explanations do not cancel each other. That forests declined and society changed dramatically in the nineteenth century is confirmable; that a single cause explains all of it on one timescale is not."),
("五、殖民接触与文物流散","1722 年，荷兰航海家罗赫芬的船队到达，欧洲人开始记录这座岛。19 世纪，传教士、商人和智利军舰相继到来；1860 年代，岛上人口因疾病和武装 raid 急剧下降。1868 年，英国军舰 Topaze 带走一尊摩艾，后来以“ Hoa Hakananai'a ”之名入藏大英博物馆。1888 年，智利正式兼并该岛。石像、木刻板和骨骸由此分散到欧洲与美洲的博物馆，岛上的社会结构也被传教、租地和殖民行政重塑。","In 1722 the Dutch navigator Roggeveen's ships reached the island, and Europeans began to record it. In the nineteenth century missionaries, traders, and Chilean warships followed; in the 1860s the population fell sharply through disease and armed raids. In 1868 the British warship Topaze removed a moai that later entered the British Museum as Hoa Hakananai'a. In 1888 Chile formally annexed the island. Figures, wood carvings, and bones were dispersed to museums in Europe and the Americas, while mission work, land leases, and colonial administration reshaped island society."),
("六、国家公园、社区与归还请求","复活节岛在 1995 年列入世界遗产，国家公园覆盖大部分遗址。今天岛上面临旅游压力、外来物种、海岸侵蚀和有限的水资源；遗址保护与拉帕努伊社区的土地、就业和自治诉求交织在一起。2018 年以来，岛民与智利政府多次要求大英博物馆归还 Hoa Hakananai'a，讨论涉及殖民取得、法律所有权和“世界博物馆”理念。石像是否“属于全人类”，与岛民是否能在自己的土地上看到祖先，是两个需要同时回答的问题。","Rapa Nui was inscribed as a World Heritage Site in 1995, with the national park covering much of the island. Today it faces tourism pressure, invasive species, coastal erosion, and limited water; site protection is intertwined with the Rapa Nui community's claims to land, employment, and self-government. Since 2018, islanders and Chilean authorities have repeatedly asked the British Museum to return Hoa Hakananai'a, a debate that touches colonial acquisition, legal title, and the idea of a 'universal museum'. Whether the figure belongs to all humanity and whether islanders can see their ancestor on their own land are questions that need answering together."),
]
