# ---------------- Antikythera ----------------
meta={
"type":"object","title_zh":"安提基特拉机械：沉船里的齿轮宇宙","title_en":"The Antikythera Mechanism: A Clockwork Cosmos from a Shipwreck",
"era":"约公元前 2 世纪–前 1 世纪","era_en":"c. 2nd–1st century BCE",
"region":"希腊，安提基特拉岛附近海域","region_en":"Waters off Antikythera, Greece",
"coords":[35.861,23.306],"tags":["安提基特拉","古希腊","天文","齿轮","考古"],"collections":[],"credibility":"verified",
"related":["lycurgus-cup","piri-reis-map"],
"en_headings":["A wreck, a crate, a geared object","Gears and dials","Who made it?","What it calculated","Research history","Museum, replicas, and open questions"],
"limits_zh":"安提基特拉机械没有留下制作者、使用地和用途的直接文字。它的齿轮数目、缺失部分和原始布局，是靠腐蚀残片、X 射线成像与碎片拼接推断出来的；不同复原方案在细节上并不一致。所谓“世界上第一台计算机”是传播用语，不是古代术语；它能计算某些天体周期，不等于能模拟全部行星运动。本文把已成像的齿轮、可读的铭文和博物馆记录写成事实，把复原模型写成有条件的解释。",
"limits_en":"The Antikythera Mechanism left no direct text naming its maker, place of use, or purpose. Its gear count, missing parts, and original layout are inferred from corroded fragments, X-ray imaging, and reconstruction; different reconstructions disagree in detail. 'The world's first computer' is a popular label, not an ancient term; the device calculated certain astronomical cycles, which is not the same as simulating all planetary motion. This entry treats imaged gears, readable inscriptions, and museum records as fact, and reconstruction models as conditional interpretation.",
}
sections=[
("一、沉船、木箱与齿轮","1900 年，希腊海绵潜水员在安提基特拉岛附近发现一艘古代沉船；1901 年，捞出的货物中有青铜与大理石雕像、玻璃器皿、钱币和一只不起眼的木箱。箱内是一组严重腐蚀的青铜齿轮与刻度盘，后来被称为“安提基特拉机械”。沉船本身约在公元前 1 世纪失事，货物来自东地中海；机械的具体来源地没有直接证据，通常与罗得岛、科林斯或叙拉古的工艺传统联系起来。","In 1900 Greek sponge divers found an ancient shipwreck near the island of Antikythera; in 1901 the recovered cargo included bronze and marble statues, glassware, coins, and an unremarkable wooden crate. Inside were severely corroded bronze gears and dials, later named the Antikythera Mechanism. The wreck itself sank around the first century BCE, and its cargo came from the eastern Mediterranean; there is no direct evidence for the device's place of origin, though it is usually linked to craft traditions of Rhodes, Corinth, or Syracuse."),
("二、齿轮与刻度","机械的核心是三十多个互相咬合的青铜齿轮，安装在木框内，正面有 zodiac 环与日历盘，背面有多个螺旋刻度盘。齿轮的齿数经过设计，用来把不同的周期换算成指针位置：太阳年、朔望月、恒星年，以及日食和月食的沙罗周期。残片上的铭文提到太阳、月亮、行星和“周期”等词。它不是一台通用计算器，而是一台专用天文模拟器：输入一个日期，输出若干天象指针的位置。","The core is more than thirty interlocking bronze gears mounted in a wooden frame, with a zodiac ring and calendar dial on the front and several spiral dials on the back. The tooth counts were designed to convert different cycles into pointer positions: the solar year, the synodic month, the sidereal year, and the Saros cycle of eclipses. Inscriptions on the fragments mention the sun, moon, planets, and 'cycles'. It was not a general-purpose calculator but a dedicated astronomical simulator: set a date, and several pointers moved to their predicted positions."),
("三、谁造的","机械没有留下制作者的名字。古代文献提到叙拉古的阿基米德制造过天球仪，也提到罗得岛的工艺传统；这些线索与机械的年代和构造相近，却不能直接证明作者。学者更倾向于把它看作一个工匠团队的产品：天文知识来自巴比伦和希腊的观测传统，齿轮工艺来自地中海东部的机械作坊。把它说成“阿基米德亲手制造”，是一种有吸引力但缺少直接证据的归属。","The device carries no maker's name. Ancient texts mention a celestial globe made by Archimedes of Syracuse and a craft tradition on Rhodes; these clues fit the device's date and construction but do not prove authorship. Scholars tend to see it as the product of a workshop: astronomical knowledge drawn from Babylonian and Greek observing traditions, gear craft from mechanical workshops in the eastern Mediterranean. Calling it 'made by Archimedes himself' is an attractive attribution without direct evidence."),
("四、它计算什么","正面外环是 zodiac 与日历，内环显示太阳和月亮的位置；背面的大螺旋盘用于显示 19 年默冬周期、18 年 11 天的沙罗周期，以及 54 年三沙罗周期。另一个小盘与四年周期的运动会有关。机械还能用一个小球表示月相。它的预测不是无限精确：齿轮的齿数、摩擦和制造误差都会累积，日食和月食也只能给出大致时间。它更像一台“天象预告器”，而不是现代意义上的导航仪或计算机。","The front outer ring carries the zodiac and calendar, with inner pointers for the sun and moon; the large spiral dial on the back displays the 19-year Metonic cycle, the 18-year 11-day Saros cycle, and the 54-year Exeligmos. Another small dial relates to the four-year cycle of athletic games. A small sphere showed the moon's phase. Its predictions were not infinitely precise: tooth counts, friction, and manufacturing error accumulate, and eclipses could only be given approximately. It was a 'sky almanac' rather than a modern navigational instrument or computer."),
("五、研究史","1902 年，考古学家 Valerios Stais 注意到齿轮；1959 年，科学史家 Derek de Solla Price 发表系统研究；1970 年代，X 射线与摄影测量推进了内部结构；2006 年，Freeth 等人在《Nature》发表 CT 重建，确认了齿轮组与天文功能；2021 年，UCL 团队在《Scientific Reports》提出“宇宙模型”复原，进一步讨论行星显示。每一次技术升级都改变了可读的齿轮数目和铭文，也改变了复原图。","In 1902 the archaeologist Valerios Stais noticed the gears; in 1959 the historian of science Derek de Solla Price published a systematic study; in the 1970s X-ray and photogrammetry advanced the internal structure; in 2006 Freeth and colleagues published a CT reconstruction in Nature confirming the gear train and astronomical function; in 2021 a UCL team proposed a 'cosmos' reconstruction in Scientific Reports, extending the discussion of planetary display. Each technical advance changed the readable gear count and inscriptions, and with them the reconstruction."),
("六、博物馆、复制品与未解之处","机械现存雅典国家考古博物馆，原件因腐蚀和脆弱很少长时间展出。研究团队制作了交互式复制品和数字模型，用于解释齿轮如何转动；这些复制品是解释工具，不是原物。仍不清楚的问题包括：它是一件孤品还是系列产品，木框和指针的原始外观，行星显示到底做到什么程度，以及它为什么随一船雕像沉入海底。沉船遗址的后续调查又发现了新的货物与船体结构，但机械的“出生地”仍是空白。","The mechanism is held in the National Archaeological Museum in Athens; the original is rarely displayed for long because of corrosion and fragility. Research teams have built interactive replicas and digital models to explain how the gears turn; these are interpretive tools, not the original. Open questions include whether it was unique or part of a series, the original appearance of the frame and pointers, how far the planetary display went, and why it sank with a cargo of statues. Later surveys of the wreck found more cargo and hull structure, but the device's 'birthplace' remains blank."),
]
