# ---------------- Rosetta Stone ----------------
meta={
"type":"object","title_zh":"罗塞塔石碑：三种文字与一把钥匙","title_en":"The Rosetta Stone: Three Scripts and a Key",
"era":"公元前 196 年","era_en":"196 BCE",
"region":"埃及，尼罗河三角洲，拉希德（罗塞塔）","region_en":"Rashid (Rosetta), Nile Delta, Egypt",
"coords":[31.404,30.416],"tags":["罗塞塔石碑","埃及学","象形文字","大英博物馆","翻译"],"collections":[],"credibility":"verified",
"related":["dead-sea-scrolls","voynich-manuscript"],
"en_headings":["A stone found in a fort","Three scripts, one decree","Young, Champollion, and the breakthrough","What the decree says","Colonial acquisition and return requests","The stone as archive and metaphor"],
"limits_zh":"石碑的发现过程、转移过程和早期释读，都有清楚的档案，但“谁第一个破译”常被简化为单人英雄故事。事实上，Young 的纸草与王名圈研究、Champollion 的科普特语比较和同时代学者的通信，共同构成了突破。石碑本身是一块残缺的诏令石，不是完整的语言教材；它提供的是三种文字并列的对照，而不是逐词翻译。本文把“可读的文本”和“后来的象征意义”分开。",
"limits_en":"The discovery, transfer, and early decipherment of the stone are well documented, but 'who decoded it first' is often reduced to a single-hero story. In fact Young's work on cartouches and papyri, Champollion's comparison with Coptic, and correspondence among contemporaries together produced the breakthrough. The stone itself is a fragmentary decree, not a complete language textbook; it offers three scripts in parallel, not a word-by-word translation. This entry separates the readable text from its later symbolic meaning.",
}
sections=[
("一、堡垒里的石头","1799 年，拿破仑埃及远征期间，法国士兵在尼罗河三角洲的拉希德（罗塞塔）附近修筑工事，拆用旧墙时发现一块黑色玄武岩板。它被运到开罗，随后在 1801 年法军向英军投降后，作为战利品运往伦敦，1802 年起在大英博物馆展出。石碑高约 112 厘米、宽约 76 厘米，顶部和底部都已残缺。它不是一座完整的碑，而是一块诏令石的残段。","In 1799, during Napoleon's Egyptian campaign, French soldiers building fortifications near Rashid (Rosetta) in the Nile Delta found a slab of black basalt while dismantling an old wall. It was taken to Cairo, then shipped to London as a prize after the French surrender to the British in 1801, and displayed in the British Museum from 1802. The stone is about 112 centimetres high and 76 centimetres wide, with both top and bottom missing. It is not a complete stele but a fragment of a decree stone."),
("二、三种文字，一份诏令","石碑表面刻着三段文字：上面是古埃及象形文字，中间是世俗体（demotic），下面是古希腊文。三段内容大致相同，是公元前 196 年孟菲斯祭司会议为托勒密五世颁布的诏令。希腊文提供了可读的入口，世俗体是当时日常使用的埃及文字，象形文字则是神庙与纪念性文本的正式书写。三段的并存，使学者第一次能用已知语言去对照未知文字。","The stone carries three texts: ancient Egyptian hieroglyphs at the top, demotic script in the middle, and Greek at the bottom. The three versions are broadly the same: a decree issued at Memphis in 196 BCE by a synod of priests in honour of Ptolemy V. Greek provided a readable entry point, demotic was the Egyptian script of daily use, and hieroglyphs were the formal script of temples and monuments. Their coexistence let scholars compare a known language with unknown scripts for the first time."),
("三、Young、Champollion 与突破","英国学者 Thomas Young 在 1810 年代研究石碑，确认王名圈中的符号可以对应希腊文人名，并提出部分象形符号的表音可能。法国学者 Jean-François Champollion 从科普特语入手，比较埃及文字与科普特语的词汇和语法；1822 年，他在《致 Dacier 先生的信》中宣布象形文字既有表意也有表音成分。这不是一个人的“灵光一现”，而是多年通信、纸草研究和同行竞争的累积。","The British scholar Thomas Young worked on the stone in the 1810s, identifying signs in royal cartouches with Greek names and suggesting that some hieroglyphs might be phonetic. The French scholar Jean-François Champollion began from Coptic, comparing Egyptian vocabulary and grammar with Coptic; in 1822, in his Lettre à M. Dacier, he announced that hieroglyphs combined ideographic and phonetic elements. This was not one person's sudden flash but the accumulation of years of correspondence, papyrus study, and rivalry."),
("四、诏令说了什么","诏令的核心是确认托勒密五世的王权与祭司特权：减免部分税收、赦免囚犯、向神庙献纳、规定祭司的服饰与仪式，并命令把诏令用三种文字刻在石碑上，分置各地。它不是文学作品，也不是哲学论文，而是一份政治—宗教行政文件。正因为内容重复、格式正式，它才适合作为语言对照的样本。石碑的价值不在“神秘”，而在“平行”。","The decree's core is the confirmation of Ptolemy V's rule and priestly privileges: tax reductions, amnesty for prisoners, offerings to temples, rules for priestly dress and ritual, and an order that the decree be inscribed in three scripts and set up in various places. It is not literature or philosophy but a political-religious administrative document. Precisely because its content is repetitive and its format formal, it suited use as a parallel-language sample. Its value lies not in mystery but in parallelism."),
("五、殖民取得与归还讨论","石碑是拿破仑远征的战争收获，后由英国取得。埃及长期要求归还，认为它是殖民掠夺的产物；大英博物馆则以“世界博物馆”与普世保存为由，强调其在全球埃及学中的位置。类似的争论也围绕贝宁青铜、帕特农雕塑展开。无论立场如何，石碑的“发现—转移—展示”链条本身就是殖民时代档案的一部分，不能只讲释读成功而不讲取得方式。","The stone was a war trophy of Napoleon's expedition and was later taken by Britain. Egypt has long requested its return, arguing that it is a product of colonial looting; the British Museum stresses its place in global Egyptology and the idea of a 'universal museum'. Similar arguments surround the Benin Bronzes and the Parthenon sculptures. Whatever the position, the chain of discovery, transfer, and display is itself part of the colonial archive; the decipherment success cannot be told without the manner of acquisition."),
("六、作为档案与隐喻","今天，石碑的文本已经被完整翻译，学者仍在研究诏令的行政细节、石碑的制作工艺和残段来源。CT 与 3D 扫描让内部结构和表面刻痕更清楚，复制品则让更多观众看到它。 “罗塞塔石碑”也成了通用隐喻，指任何提供关键对照、打开未知领域的事物。这个隐喻很方便，却容易让人忘记：石碑只提供了一段有限的对照文本，真正的破译靠的是此后几代人的纸草、铭文和语法研究。","Today the stone's text has been fully translated, while scholars still study the decree's administrative details, the stone's manufacture, and the provenance of the fragment. CT and 3D scanning make the internal structure and surface cuts clearer, and replicas let more visitors see it. 'Rosetta Stone' has also become a general metaphor for anything that provides a key comparison and opens an unknown field. The metaphor is convenient but tends to obscure the fact that the stone supplied only a limited parallel text; actual decipherment depended on generations of papyrus, inscription, and grammar research."),
]
